<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="995b3ede">&lt;p&gt;
    Pairwise Testing is a combinatorial method of testing&amp;nbsp;that determines the minimum number of scenarios needed
    in&amp;nbsp;order to test all discrete pairs of input parameters at least once. This technique may also be referred to as
    all pairs testing, pair testing&amp;nbsp;or two-way testing.&amp;nbsp;This approach to test design is especially useful when
    faced with:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Too many tests
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Unknown test coverage
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Scattered and outdated documentation
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        High costs for both execution and automation
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Pairwise Testing helps to provide:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Better test coverage&lt;/strong&gt; by maximizing the scope being tested while minimizing the various number of
        test cases required to do so
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Increased quality of test cases&lt;/strong&gt; by maximizing variations and eliminating redundancies during test
        case creation
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Ease of managing change requests&lt;/strong&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Pairwise Testing can support a variety of situations such as during:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Initial testing&lt;/strong&gt;, when there is:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        A large number of configuration options to validate
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Complex functionalities requiring unit tests
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        A significant number of existing test cases that will be affected by a requirement change
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Regression(s) requiring a review and prioritization of test coverage
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;System Integration Testing / User Acceptance Testing,&lt;/strong&gt; when there are:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        End to end test scenarios that need to be identified and optimized based on known factors
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        System Integration Tests (SITs) whose test coverage needs to be reviewed and prioritized
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Production / future releases,&lt;/strong&gt; when:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Existing test flows need to be reviewed and utilized to determine applicable regression suite for subsequent
        releases
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Existing test design approach needs to be leveraged to create optimal test designs for future releases
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    For more information on Pairwise Testing and supporting tool(s), such as Hexawise, please refer to the &lt;a
    class=&quot;elementLinkWithUserText&quot;
    href=&quot;./../../../practice.ita.delv.it_test.base/guidances/supportingmaterials/intro_pairwise_testing_39F49C40.html&quot;
    guid=&quot;_BQNswES1EeiASMlqgAcI7A&quot;&gt;Pairwise Testing deck&lt;/a&gt;.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
